# 📄 ODT Files — Beginner Notes

## What is an ODT file?

ODT stands for **OpenDocument Text**.

It is a document format used by applications such as LibreOffice Writer. It can contain text, images, tables, formatting and metadata.

## An ODT file is also a ZIP archive

Although it looks like a single document, an ODT file contains several files and folders packaged together.

You can inspect these contents without opening the document in a word processor.

## 1. Identify the file type

In Terminal:

```bash
file "document.odt"
```

The `file` command examines the file’s contents to identify its type.

A filename extension alone does not guarantee the actual file type.

## 2. Read its metadata with ExifTool

```bash
exiftool "document.odt"
```

Depending on the document, this may reveal:
- Creator or author
- Creation and modification dates
- Editing software
- Document title
- Other document properties

Not every document contains all these details.

## 3. List the contents without extracting

```bash
unzip -l "document.odt"
```

The `-l` option lists the files inside the archive.

Common entries include:

| File or folder | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `content.xml` | Document text and content structure |
| `meta.xml` | Metadata such as creator and dates |
| `styles.xml` | Formatting and styles |
| `settings.xml` | Application settings |
| `Pictures/` | Embedded images, when present |
| `META-INF/manifest.xml` | List of package contents and their media types |
| `mimetype` | Identifies the document format |

## 4. Read the metadata XML directly

```bash
unzip -p "document.odt" meta.xml
```

The `-p` option prints an archived file’s contents into Terminal without extracting it.

For easier reading on macOS:

```bash
unzip -p "document.odt" meta.xml | xmllint --format -
```

- `|` sends the output of the first command into the next.
- `xmllint --format` formats the XML.
- The final `-` tells xmllint to read that incoming output.

Possible metadata tags include:

| XML tag | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `<meta:initial-creator>` | Initial creator recorded in the document |
| `<dc:creator>` | Creator associated with the latest modification |
| `<meta:creation-date>` | Recorded creation date |
| `<dc:date>` | Recorded last modification date |
| `<meta:generator>` | Software that generated the document |

## 5. Inspect the document content

```bash
unzip -p "document.odt" content.xml | xmllint --format -
```

This displays the XML structure containing the document’s text.

It is not a clean plain-text version: XML tags and formatting information remain visible.

## 6. Extract the contents into a folder

```bash
unzip "document.odt" -d "document_extracted"
```

The `-d` option chooses the destination folder.

You can then inspect the extracted files:

```bash
ls "document_extracted"
```

On Mac, open the folder in Finder:

```bash
open "document_extracted"
```

There is no need to rename the ODT file to `.zip` before using these commands.

## Investigation reminders

- Work on a copy and preserve the original.
- Metadata can be edited or removed.
- An author field is a clue, not proof of who wrote the document.
- Embedded images may have their own metadata.
- Password-protected documents may prevent inspection of encrypted contents.
- Review extracted information before sharing it publicly.

## Quick reference

| Goal | Command |
| --- | --- |
| Identify the file | `file "document.odt"` |
| Read metadata | `exiftool "document.odt"` |
| List packaged files | `unzip -l "document.odt"` |
| Read metadata XML | `unzip -p "document.odt" meta.xml` |
| Extract the package | `unzip "document.odt" -d "document_extracted"` |
